Haiku: keep a new window's tab on the screen; report un-zoom as restored

SDL places a window by its content area, and the Haiku backend made that
the BWindow's frame as it was. The tab and the border lie outside it, and
Haiku moves no window by itself: a window as large as the screen, or one
at y = 0 (SDL_WINDOWPOS_CENTERED does that for a window as high as the
screen), had its tab above the top of the screen, where it cannot be
dragged, zoomed or closed. Blender's render window (the render resolution,
clamped to the screen size and centred on the mouse) opened that way.

Windows with a border now keep their tab on the screen when they are
created, moved, or leave fullscreen; one that cannot fit is shrunk if it is
resizable. SDL learns the new frame before SDL_CreateWindow() returns.
Windows placed elsewhere, borderless and fullscreen windows are left alone.

The zoom button: BWindow::Zoom() calls the Zoom() hook both to zoom and to
zoom back, and the hook reported both as SDL_WINDOWEVENT_MAXIMIZED, so
SDL_WINDOW_MAXIMIZED never cleared and SDL_MaximizeWindow() toggled. The
un-zoom (and SDL_RestoreWindow() of a zoomed window) is now reported as
SDL_WINDOWEVENT_RESTORED, which the handler did not handle at all (also not
for an unminimized window), and a maximize request zooms only a window that
is not zoomed.

